[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/ger/81mm_sGrW34]
8cm schwere Granatwerfer 34 (8cm Mortar model 1934 s.Gr.W.34)

The medium battalion mortar s.Gr.W.34 (heavy by German classification) was designed by Rheinmetall AG in 1932. Technically it was based on the Stocks-Brandt mortar, employing what would become the classical quasi-triangle design (in which the barrel and bipod formed two sides of the triangle, with the ground serving as the third side).

The mortar weighed 57 kg, and could be easily disassembled into three parts for transport. In addition to the main design, a light mortar for airborne forces was built, which weighed only 26 kg. The mortar's ammunition included two types of common fragmentation mines, as well as bounding fragmentation mines, and smoke and target-marking mines. Remarkably, German mines could be fired from Soviet 82mm mortars, whereas Soviet mines could not be fired from 81mm German mortars.

The 81mm mortars were used in machine gun companies of infantry battalions, with 6 mortars per battalion, and sometimes even more. These s.Gr.W.34 mortars were widely used throughout the war and earned the reputation of a simple, reliable and effective weapon. A total of 71,630 mortars of this type were serially produced during the war years (from 1939 to 1945).

The s.Gr.W.34 was sometimes installed on specifically modified armoured personnel carriers, the SdKfz 250 and SdKfz 251 (the SdKfz 250/7 and SdKfz 251/2 respectively), which were used in panzergrenadier regiments.

[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/ger/SdKfz_251_2_Ausf_C_Kursk1943]
SdKfz 251/2 Ausf.C (80mm s.Gr.W.34 Mortar Carriers based on Medium Half-truck Personnel Carrier SdKfz 251C)

The success of the SdKfz 251 medium personnel carrier led to its adoption as a basis for the construction of a wide range of specialized vehicles, including the SdKfz 251/2 mortar carrier. The base design was changed only slightly: an 8cm s.Gr.W.34 mortar was mounted in the vehicle body, and it was equipped with ammunition boxes. The front machine gun was usually removed to accommodate. Combat crew included the vehicle commander, the driver, and the mortar crew. The mortar could easily be dismounted if needed, but it was also possible to fire from the vehicle.

The SdKfz 251/2 entered the service of heavy companies of mechanized battalions of panzergrenadier regiments, under the command of tank and panzergrenadier divisions of the Wehrmacht and the SS. The vehicle combined high manoeuvrability with reasonable ballistic protection for the crew. It is universally agreed that the vehicle was a highly successful design. Until September 1943, mortar carriers were produced on the bases of SdKfz 251 Ausf.C, which took part in the Battle of Kursk. Then, from the autumn of 1943, the Ausf.D modification was put into serial production. The SdKfz 251/2 carriers were used on all fronts until the end of the war.

[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/usa/81mm_mortar_M1]
81mm Mortar M1

The World War I quickly became a trench war, and naturally it was difficult to capture systems of trenches covered with barbed wire and supported by machine gun fire. Under these conditions, the critical weapon became the mortar – a light and cheap overhead fire weapon. Of the numerous variants, the one supplied by the British engineer Wilfred Stokes was the most successful, and its quasi-triangle form has dominated mortar design ever since.
Upon entering World War I, the US Army adopted the 3-inch Stokes mortar, and used it actively from that moment forward.
After the war, the Stokes mortar was upgraded by the French engineer Edgar Brandt, who invented the water-drop shaped mortar projectile. Thus, the Stokes-Brand design became the classic mortar.
In 1931, the US Army purchased four Stokes-Brandt mortars, which had been adopted by the French forces as the mle 1927/31. After some tests and improvements, the Army adopted it as the M1.
M1 mortars were categorized as battalion artillery, and saw active use in World War II, Korea, and Vietnam, again and again proving their efficiency and reliability. The mortar had a wide range of ammunition, including several types of HE shells, smoke, and illumination rounds, which further expanded its application. The M1 mortar was quite close to its Soviet counterpart, but it had a smaller elevation angle. There was also a self-propelled variant based on the M3 half-track.
From the late 1950s forward, the US Army gradually replaced the M1 with the lighter and longer-range M29.

[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/usa/m21_halftrack_mortar]
M21 Mortar Motor Carriage (MMC) - self-propelled artillery mount on a half-track chassis.

It was produced by the White Motor Company in 1944. Only 110 examples were produced. It mainly served on the Western Front in Normandy, and Southern France, and then later in the Ardennes during the Battle of the Bulge. It was deemed to have been outclassed in 1945 when the 81 mm M1 mortar was found to have insufficient power to provide effective artillery support.

The M4 was developed to meet the need for a specialized mortar carrier based on the M2 Half Track Car, which the M21 soon replaced. The initial M4 was designed to carry a mortar. In emergencies, the mortar could be fired from the vehicle. The improved M4A1 had a reinforced floor so that the mortar could be fired from the vehicle, but the mounting faced to the rear and lacked traverse. This caused problems when the troops who operated the mortar mounted the vehicle, so the US 2nd Armored Division relocated the mortars so that they could fire from the front.

The Ordnance Department followed suit and a new 81 mm MMC, the T19 MMC, was created, which was based on the larger M3 Half-track. Developmental trials of the T19 were completed in July 1943, and the vehicle was later standardized as the M21 MMC.

[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/rus/50mm_mortar_obr1941]
50mm Company Mortar model 1941

Design of the 50mm company mortar began in the beginning of 1937 at the design bureau of Plant No. 7. After the trials of several prototypes, the 50mm company mortar model 1938, based on a quasi-triangle design scheme, was accepted for service. Further modernization led to the 50mm company mortar model 1940, which was easier to manufacture.

The 50mm company mortar model 1941 used a sealed scheme, meaning that all its parts were mounted on a base plate. The barrel had only two fixed angles of fire – 45 and 75 degrees – and in addition to this the mine had only one charge. Firing range was adjusted with the help of a special sleeve on the barrel, which served as an escape for the part of the gases, which changed the barrel pressure. Ammunition consisted of two types of mines, one with six fins and the other with ten. In combat and on the move the mortar was transported by one crew member, either by hand or in a special backpack. Mines were transported in racks, with seven mines in each. The mortar design was smart and every bit as good as those of other countries, such as the German G.W.35 50mm mortar.

Organizationally, 50mm mortars were used in rifle companies, each of which included three mortars. From December 1941 through July 1942, these mortars were removed from the companies and battalions and were consolidated into regimental mortar battalions. From December 1944 onward, 50mm mortars were removed from the armaments of rifle divisions.

50mm mortars were widely used throughout the war, though by the end of the war their number decreased significantly due the termination of production. Almost 175,000 company mortars were produced in 1939-1943.

[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/rus/82mm_mortar_obr1941]
82mm Battalion Mortar M1941

The USSR began work on mortars in the mid-1920s. Most of these projects were developed by the team known as "Group D" under the supervision of N. Dorovlyov. However, none of these entered mass production until the mid-1930s.
In 1929, in the armed conflicts on the Chinese Eastern Railway, Soviet troops captured a few 81mm Stokes-Brandt mortars which featured the classic quasi-triangle design. By the end of 1931, Group D had developed an 82mm mortar based on these trophies. Field tests, which initially utilized captured Chinese shells, were launched in 1933, but these tests proved to be time-consuming. In 1936, the first Soviet mortar, designated the M1936 82mm battalion mortar, was adopted and entered mass production. Shortly thereafter, a newer variant was launched – the M1937 82mm battalion mortar – which replaced the older model's square baseplate with a round plate.
Improvements continued, and the next variant – the M1941 82mm battalion mortar – had detachable wheels and a different bipod and base plate. The third and final wartime variant – the M1943 82mm battalion mortar – had a different bipod, wheels, and a gun sight mount. They were each so popular that all three variants were produced simultaneously during the war.
The M1941 was based on the quasi-triangle design, and thanks to detachable wheels, the assembled mortar could be quickly redeployed across short distances by a single soldier. Wheels were often detached during firing. The mortar could also be disassembled into three parts and redeployed by the crew. Ammunition included fragmentation and smoke shells of six and ten fins (with different charges). The mortar was also capable of firing captured German and lend-leased US shells. Overall, the M1941 82mm battalion mortar established an excellent combat reputation and was highly manufacturable.

82mm mortars were categorized under battalion artillery, but the number of units per battalion varied. Before July 1941, each battalion had a mortar company with six 82mm mortars. But in July 1941, this was changed to a single mortar squad with two mortars. Finally, in December 1941, mortars were excluded from rifle battalions and organized into regimental mortar battalions. From July 1942 through the end of the war, each battalion had a company with nine mortars, and a motorized rifle battalion had six 82mm mortars. Battalion mortars were actively used during the war, and their mass production in 1939-45 totaled 168 thousand pieces. For a long time during the postwar years, various 82mm mortar variants remained in Soviet Army service. Many more mortars were exported, to North Korea in particular, which put them to active use in the Korean War.

[EncDescription_data/units/mortar/UK/81mm_Ordnance_ML_3_inch_Mortar]
Ordnance ML 3-inch Mortar Mk.I

During the first World War, the British quickly learned to appreciate the mortar, and actively used the Stokes Mortar, which was based on the quasitriangle design. Modified by Brandt after the war, the mortar now known as the Stokes-Brandt gained worldwide recognition, and has seen wide use even to this day.

The British modification of the Stokes-Brandt Mortar, designated the Ordnance ML 3-inch mortar Mk. I, was adopted in the second half of the 1920s, and was actively used throughout World War II. The British mortar had a shorter range than its German counterpart, a problem that was remedied by the Mk. II modification, which saw active service into the 1960s.

